A||||3||Avery Montembeault||part information needed||||1||
Z||000000||Avery Montembeault||12-30-2001||11:11 PM||||this may seem trivial, but do the brass washer things used where the brake line connects to the caliper (its an 88 model caliper) go by any special name, or is it simply "brass caliper washer". Ive been told you should really only use them once, as they form a seal then the screw compresses them. well, Ive used mine about half a dozen times and its showing in that my caliper gurgles a little fluid. Any ideas what parts store would readily have them in stock?||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||12-30-2001||11:23 PM||||I guess I would try a NAPA or Carquest, possibly even a dealer.  Asking for brass caliper hydraulic line washers and bringing in your old one should be enough info for them to find you some.  I have installed rebuilt and new calipers on both my 87 coupe and the 84 SE I restored, and they all came with the wrong size washers.  They would all leak, and I didn't want to tighten the line up any more for fear of breaking the line screw.  Out of necessity, I had to re-use the old ones, and they haven't leaked yet.<P>But, in your case, you actually <I>need</I> new ones because they are leaking.  Try any reputable parts places, they should know what you need and be able to find something for you.||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||12-31-2001||09:02 AM||||The Help line of part has them.  I found them at a NAPA store but anywhere should have them.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||12-31-2001||09:21 AM||||I've always heard them referred to as "<B>crush washers</B>" because the soft metal they are made of gets compressed to seal the fitting they are used on.||||reg||1
